I didn't have any sleep items (I know I haven't seen any wands of
such, and am fairly certain about having never seen a potion...). 
Vampire Lords don't eat, so I couldn't distract it with food.  I don't
carry cursed items around, generally (especially since I accidentally
blew up my BoH).  Vamp Lords also fly, so pits wouldn't be very
helpful either.  There may, indeed, have been ways for me to keep the
priest alive, but that requires my a) realizing that Vinny was
powerful enough to take him on (priests being fairly tough, if I
remember correctly), and b) having some way to lock him up/put him to
sleep, and whistle him around the temple.  Sometimes you'll have the
necessary stuff, sometimes you won't.  I don't think I did.

Regardless, this seems to me to be a bug.  In RL (yes, NHINRL, but...)
pets are generally trained to not attack people whom their owners are
peaceful towards (my dog won't attack my sister; guard dogs will only
attack strange people intruding on the territory they're guarding,
etc.)  Yes, a poorly-trained guard dog may need to be locked away from
strangers, but that's "poorly-trained".  If my dog even barks at
someone I don't want him to, I can reprimand him.  What justification
is there for NH pets attacking anything and everything they can?  I
can't see any, other than giving players a method of stealing from
shops and getting their money back from priests without any negative
ramifications (Kops, "You murderer!", etc.).  And, of course, chaotics
may want their pets to act that way (though I think they should be
held responsible for their pet's actions, regardless), but lawfuls
should strive to not kill peaceful creatures (or let them be killed). 
(Neutrals, of course, fall somewhere in the middle.)

The way it seems to me that pets should work would be for them to not
attack monsters who are peaceful towards their owner.  If the owner
takes a swing at a shopkeeper, the shopkeeper instantly becomes
hostile (this is true of the game as it is now), and so the pet would
naturally turn to protect his owner.  If the owner reads a scroll of
taming (or similar action) and makes a hostile creature peaceful
(without taming), the pet would realize that it's no longer a threat,
and thus stop attacking (as it currently stops attacking tamed
creatures).  If a player makes a shopkeeper/priest/whatever hostile,
and then leaves his pet to finish the formerly peaceful one off, the
game should treat it as if the player had killed him (hold the player
responsible for his pet's actions--which is in most legal systems
today), and punish him accordingly.

As an example, consider my earlier recounting of my morning
experience, and compare with this: when Thvor found Crom's temple this
morning, she (an unfortunate incident with an amulet) had a pet Master
Vampire (Vinny).  Vinny, being a loyal pet, should not attack the
priest.  Thvor then trys to buy protection and fails, and, in a burst
of rage, attacks the priest; the priest goes hostile, and Vinny joins
the fray.  Thvor realizes that she's weaker than the priest, and runs
for the exit, but Vinny, being as powerful as he is, stays and
continues to attack.  The priest dies.  Crom, being the deity that he
is, isn't happy about his priest being killed, and thus seeks
retribution.  Knowing that Vinny is just a pet, he directs his wrath
against Thvor, stripping her of her protection, etc., etc.

This scenario seems _much_ more likely and realistic to me, and thus
is what I'd expect the game to do (and thus non-buggy).  I plan on
making my version of the game do so, and posting the patch online, so
anyone who agrees can use it as well; perhaps it will be submitted
to/picked up by the dev team, and worked into the game, perhaps not. 
That's the way of patches.

